<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Largo Caballero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Largo Caballero was a Spanish trade unionist and Socialist leader, head of the UGT union, whom admirers called 'the Spanish Lenin'. He was prime minister of the Second Republic from September 1936 to May 1937, bringing anarchist ministers into government, but fell after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona-may-days">Barcelona May Days</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1946 after imprisonment in the Sachsenhausen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-largo-caballero.jpg" alt="Francisco Largo Caballero" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agence de presse Meurisse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Largo_Caballero_1927_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francisco Largo Caballero was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 15 October 1869. He left school at the age of seven, was largely self-educated and made his living as a stuccoer of walls. He took part in a construction workers' strike in 1890 and joined the Spanish Socialist Workers' Party (PSOE) in 1894. In his early career he concentrated on municipal politics and burial societies and disapproved of the methods of the anarchist union, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederacion-nacional-del-trabajo">Confederación Nacional del Trabajo</a> (CNT). When the party's founder Pablo Iglesias died in 1925, Largo Caballero became head of the party and of the Workers' General Union (UGT).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He began as a moderate. He advocated a degree of cooperation between the UGT and the military government of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>, which let the union go on working, and this began his long conflict with Indalecio Prieto, who opposed any collaboration. From 1931 to 1933 he was Minister of Labour in the first governments of the Second Republic. He tried to improve the lot of landless labourers in the rural south, setting up mixed juries to arbitrate in agrarian disputes and introducing an eight-hour day in the countryside.</p><p>After the right-wing CEDA won the elections of 19 November 1933, he turned sharply left. In January 1934 he wrote in El Socialista, Harmony? No! Class war! The historian Angel Smith judges his radicalism almost purely rhetorical. In October 1934 he was one of the leaders of the failed workers' rising, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, which was put down by the government. It damaged him politically, and Prieto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> gained the initiative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> won the elections of February 1936 and the military coup of 17 July began the civil war, President Azaña asked Largo Caballero to form a government on 4 September 1936. He was prime minister and also minister of war, and on 4 November he persuaded the CNT to join the government, with four members in junior ministries. During his term he grew disenchanted with the communists and clashed with the Soviet ambassador Marcel Rosenberg.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona-may-days">Barcelona May Days</a> of 3 to 8 May 1937 caused a crisis. His attempt to defend the POUM, one of the parties involved, set moderate Socialist ministers such as Prieto and the Communists against him, and he resigned on 17 May 1937; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-negrin">Juan Negrín</a> succeeded him. After the Republic's defeat in 1939 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was arrested during the German occupation and held in the Sachsenhausen camp from 1943 to 1945. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1946, and his remains were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1978.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Largo Caballero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the war the Republic had three governments, led by José Giral, <b>Francisco Largo Caballero</b> and Juan Negrín.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It was later broadened to include other Republican parties and was recognised by <b>Largo Caballero</b>'s government in December 1936, though Communists and Republicans criticised it.</blockquote><a href="/state/regional-defence-council-of-aragon">Regional Defence Council of Aragon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco Largo Caballero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
